(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-acute-vision-loss-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Acute Vision Loss Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old man with angle-closure glaucoma is about to start a medication for overactive bladder. Which counselling point applies?","choices":["Anticholinergic drugs will lower his pressure","Anticholinergic drugs are safe in any glaucoma","Anticholinergic drugs cause optic neuritis","Anticholinergic drugs cause papilledema","Anticholinergic drugs can worsen his glaucoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; the lecture names systemic anticholinergics such as atropine. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man reports that vision in his left eye went completely dark for about six hours yesterday before returning. Why does this history argue against a transient ischemic attack?","choices":["A transient ischemic attack affects both eyes","A transient ischemic attack never recovers","A transient ischemic attack spares the retina","A transient ischemic attack lasts seconds to minutes","A transient ischemic attack is always painful"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Jaquith was explicit that hours puts it outside that window. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with carotid disease describes vision in one eye going dark instantly two hours ago and not returning. He has no pain. The retina is pale. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Amaurosis fugax","Central retinal vein occlusion","Optic neuritis","Angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; profound painless loss over seconds that does not recover. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy takes an antihypertensive at bedtime. Which change may be considered?","choices":["Adding a topical beta-blocker","Moving the dose away from bedtime","Doubling the bedtime dose","Stopping all antihypertensives","Adding a corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, to avoid nocturnal hypotension, which can worsen the condition. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman asks which retinal vascular occlusion is seen more often. What does the lecture state?","choices":["They occur equally often","Neither occurs after age 50","Only artery occlusion occurs in diabetes","Vein occlusion is more common","Artery occlusion is more common"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, more common than central retinal artery occlusion. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man loses vision in one eye. Which finding separates arterial from venous retinal occlusion on fundoscopy?","choices":["A cherry-red spot rather than haemorrhages","Disc cupping rather than swelling","An elevated grey retina","Blurred disc margins in both eyes","A hazy cornea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the artery gives pallor with a cherry-red fovea. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman had cataract extraction last year and is highly myopic. Which condition is she at increased risk of?","choices":["Amaurosis fugax","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Papilledema","Angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; myopia and cataract extraction are both listed risk factors. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman says a dark cloud has settled over the upper part of her vision in one eye. She notices it shifts when she lies down. There is no pain and no headache. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Papilledema","Central retinal artery occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Optic neuritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the detached retina floats, so the defect moves with head position. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has sudden painless monocular vision loss and no other findings. Which single step does the lecture treat as non-negotiable?","choices":["An eye patch and analgesia","Repeat visual acuity in the morning","Urgent ophthalmology involvement","A trial of topical antibiotics","Reassurance and review in a week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; each of these diagnoses is sight-threatening and time-dependent. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had blurred vision in her left eye worsening over three days, and it hurts when she moves the eye. Colours look washed out. The disc appears normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Painful eye movement, colour desaturation and an often normal disc. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is told he has papilledema. Which pressure is raised?","choices":["Intraocular pressure","Central venous pressure","Arterial blood pressure","Orbital compartment pressure","Intracranial pressure"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; the lecturer drew a sharp line against intraocular pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has painful acute vision loss. Which diagnosis does the pain point toward?","choices":["Amaurosis fugax","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion","Central retinal vein occlusion","Retinal detachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; the occlusions and detachment are painless. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man presents six hours after a central retinal artery occlusion. Which option remains available per the lecture?","choices":["Carotid endarterectomy that evening","Thrombolytic infusion into the ophthalmic artery","Laser peripheral iridotomy","Scleral buckle","Oral prednisone taper"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, within eight hours of onset. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with hypertension, diabetes and obstructive sleep apnea has sudden painless loss of the lower half of vision in one eye. He has no headache, scalp tenderness or jaw pain. His discs are described as small and crowded. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion","Retinal detachment","Non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The disc at risk with vascular comorbidity and no arteritic features. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man describes a curtain descending over the vision of his right eye. It lasted about 40 seconds, there was no pain, and his sight returned completely. He has hypertension and carotid bruits. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Amaurosis fugax"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Transient monocular loss lasting seconds to minutes, then full recovery. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman took an over-the-counter antihistamine and hours later developed a severe unilateral headache, a red eye and vomiting. Her pupil is mid-dilated and her cornea looks cloudy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Migraine with aura","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; anticholinergic drugs precipitate it. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman and a 74-year-old man both have monocular vision loss with an abnormal optic nerve. Which feature most favours optic neuritis over ischemic optic neuropathy?","choices":["Pain on eye movement","A pale, swollen disc","Onset over seconds","Scalp tenderness","A raised sedimentation rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the ischemic neuropathies are painless. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has sudden painless loss of a wedge of vision in one eye, with pallor limited to one arterial territory. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Branch retinal artery occlusion","Central retinal artery occlusion","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Retinal detachment","Amaurosis fugax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; a branch is blocked rather than the main trunk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has chronic open-angle glaucoma that continues to progress on maximal drops. Which does the lecture give next?","choices":["Observation alone","Systemic corticosteroids","Carotid endarterectomy","Laser trabeculoplasty","A stronger prostaglandin analogue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, used when the disease is refractory or advanced. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man needs repair of a retinal detachment. Which does the lecture list as an option?","choices":["Temporal artery biopsy","Anterior chamber paracentesis","Scleral buckle","Peripheral iridotomy","Trabeculoplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with vitrectomy, pneumatic retinopexy, cryotherapy and laser photocoagulation. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with sudden monocular vision loss and no other neurological findings asks why he is being worked up so aggressively. Which principle did the lecturer stress twice?","choices":["Sudden vision loss is a stroke only with weakness","Sudden vision loss rarely needs referral","Sudden vision loss is usually migraine","Sudden vision loss is a stroke until proven otherwise","Sudden vision loss is benign if it resolves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A stroke can present with acute vision loss and nothing else. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman is treated for arteritic optic neuropathy. Which test does the lecture call the gold standard for the underlying arteritis?","choices":["MRI of the orbits","Fluorescein angiography","Temporal artery biopsy","C-reactive protein","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man says a curtain came across his vision. Which additional detail best separates retinal detachment from amaurosis fugax?","choices":["Whether he is over 50","Whether the curtain lifted within minutes","Whether the eye was painful","Whether one eye was affected","Whether he has hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amaurosis fugax resolves; a detachment persists and advances. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man's transient monocular vision loss is traced to a carotid embolus with significant plaque. Which intervention does the lecture pair with that finding?","choices":["Carotid endarterectomy","Calcium channel blockers","Laser peripheral iridotomy","Intravenous methylprednisolone","Scleral buckle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers are reserved for vascular spasm. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old African American man has no eye symptoms. Routine screening shows an increased cup-to-disc ratio, splinter haemorrhages at the rim and a bilateral pressure of 26 mmHg. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Papilledema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic, with optic nerve changes on an open angle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. What share of anterior ischemic optic neuropathy does this form represent?","choices":["5 to 10 percent","Under 5 percent","90 to 95 percent","50 to 60 percent","25 to 30 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it presents at 40 to 60 years of age. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with optic neuritis asks whether his sight will return. What does the lecture say?","choices":["Vision usually recovers spontaneously","Vision loss is permanent","Vision returns only after surgery","Vision worsens over years","Recovery requires lifelong drops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; it begins improving within weeks and is usually normal within a year. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has acute angle closure and a very high intraocular pressure. Which does the lecture give as the intravenous agent, followed by mannitol or isosorbide?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Alteplase","Famotidine","Acetazolamide","Methylprednisolone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor used to drop the pressure rapidly. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has weeks of headache, nausea and vomiting with flickering and intermittent blurred vision. Both optic discs are swollen with blurred margins and engorged veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Papilledema"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral disc swelling with signs of raised intracranial pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has had a headache worst on waking for six weeks, with brief greying of vision when she stands. Both discs are elevated with engorged veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Central retinal vein occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Papilledema"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; bilateral disc swelling with the symptoms of raised intracranial pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
